{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795287463,"nonce":"oN9HAlr78uZNFbeh0tm7RjTFKds=","message":"Partial fix committed locally as b9115609d7c8c00f7fd047016eddb7b7cb8aa6c3 (not pushed). Issue stays open: the root question below is not answered.\n\nCause found for most of the gap: loop_threshold was set from opt_threshold, so the game preset (opt 200) sent any loop to LLVM after 200 iterations. The extra LLVM functions under game were mostly warm loops, not dense ones: BroadPhase.insertLeaf/balance/fixUpwards (tree walks), Epa.*, attachShape. A preset can now carry its own loop threshold. Game's is 10 000, its call threshold stays 200. A typed --opt-threshold still sets both. ASH_LOOP_THRESHOLD overrides for experiments.\n\nNUC (Linux x86_64, cores 0,2,4,6), HingedChainBench best ms per step, median of interleaved runs, vs application:\n\n    20 chains (15 runs)   game loops=200  +14.2%   loops=5000  +3.8%   loops=10000  +2.0%\n    80 chains (15 runs)   game loops=200   +4.1%   loops=5000  -3.7%   loops=10000  -3.9%\n    final binary, plain --preset game (10 runs)\n      20 chains  -0.6%      80 chains  +2.3%\n      same binary with --opt-threshold 200 (loops follow):  +6.4% and +13.4%\n\nRun-to-run noise is about 4%, so read the result as game now matching application, not beating it.\n\nCompute and pointer-heavy programs, whole process, hybrid, game vs application (7 runs):\n\n    nbody -0.4%   spectralnorm +1.1%   fannkuch +1.8%   deltablue +0.5%   binary_trees +0.4%\n\nEarlier sweep, application vs game vs Cranelift-only (whole process, vs application): spectralnorm Cranelift +68%, nbody +6%, mandelbrot +6%, binary_trees +11%, deltablue -7.6%, fannkuch -2.7%. LLVM matters for dense loops. Game's early LLVM did not beat application on any of them.\n\nStill open:\n- Cranelift-only is still fastest on this physics step (about 1.69 ms against 1.78 for application). Some LLVM versions run slower than their Cranelift versions. Which function, and why, is not known. There is no knob to refuse LLVM for one findex.\n- Promotion by loop shape, so dense array and SIMD loops reach LLVM first, is not done.\n- --jit-tier llvm at 34.7 ms per step is 20x slower than hybrid on this program.\n- Not confirmed on any other game-shaped program.","files":null}]}